.TH MARIO 6 "2026-08-26" "mario" "Games Manual"
.SH NAME
mario \- terminal Mario-style platformer with an online leaderboard
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B mario
.RI [ options ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B mario
is a Super Mario Bros.-style platformer that renders as square half-block
pixels in a terminal (or canvas pixels in the browser build).
The engine is fully deterministic \[em] identical input sequences reproduce
identical games \[em] and every leaderboard submission carries the run's
input recording, which a verifier replays to prove the score.
.PP
Seven levels are built in (overworld, underground, sky and castle themes),
plus a deterministic daily challenge generated from the date.
Power states: super mushroom, fire flower (max two fireballs), and star
power. Stomping enemies in a chain scores escalating points and eventually
extra lives.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-demo ", " \-demoticks\ N
Run a headless scripted demo for
.I N
ticks (default 6000) and exit.
.TP
.BI \-level\ FILE
Play a custom ASCII level instead of the built-ins.
.TP
.BI \-width\ N
Viewport width in tiles (0 = terminal width).
.TP
.BR \-basic
Force 16-color ANSI output instead of truecolor.
.TP
.BI \-scores\ N
Print the top
.I N
leaderboard scores and exit.
.TP
.BR \-daily
Play today's daily challenge (with
.BR \-scores ,
print the daily board).
.TP
.BI \-ui\-preview\ MODE
Render a leaderboard UI screen headless and exit
.RB ( ask ", " entry ", " board ", " title-board ).
.TP
.BI \-serve\ ADDR
Run an unauthenticated SSH game server on
.I ADDR
(e.g.
.BR :2222 )
instead of playing locally: anyone can connect with
.B "ssh \-t anyuser@host \-p 2222"
and play. The server implements the SSH transport, curve25519 key
exchange and session channels itself (stdlib only); there is no shell,
exec or forwarding \[em] the game is the only service. Each connection
gets its own player identity for the leaderboard.
.TP
.BI \-hostkey\ PATH
With
.BR \-serve :
persist the host key at
.I PATH
(created on first use). Without it every launch generates a fresh key,
so clients see a new host fingerprint each time.
.TP
.BR \-verify\-pending
Service tool: replay-verify pending scores (requires the service key).
.SH CONTROLS
.TS
tab(|);
l l.
a/d or left/right|move
w or space|jump
x|run (hold), fireball (fire flower)
p|pause
r|restart (after game over / win)
k|suicide (die on demand when trapped)
q|quit
l|leaderboard (on the title screen)
d|daily challenge (on the title screen)
.TE
.SH LEADERBOARD
After game over the game offers to submit your score to a Supabase-backed
online board. Rows are replay-verified: an automated verifier re-executes
each submission's recorded inputs and deletes rows that do not reproduce
their claimed score. Verified rows show a check mark.
.SH SSH SERVER
.B "mario \-serve"
accepts any username with the
.B none
authentication method and serves one game session per connection over a
pty. Output is truecolor by default
.RB ( \-basic
for 16 colors); the viewport is sized from the client's pty. Keystrokes
arrive as raw terminal bytes, so the kitty keyboard protocol and legacy
key-repeat inference both work over SSH. Concurrent sessions are capped
(default 16).
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B SUPABASE_URL ", " SUPABASE_KEY
Leaderboard endpoint and publishable key (also read from a
.I .env
file in the working directory).
.TP
.B SUPABASE_SERVICE_KEY
Service key for
.BR \-verify\-pending .
.SH FILES
None. The native binary writes nothing to the user's machine: the player
identity is per-run (a fresh UUID each launch). The browser build keeps
its identity and local best in localStorage under
.I mario.player
only.
